\section{The tests}{
A point statement names the null hypothesis H0; an inequality or a region
names the alternative H1, as in \code{\link[=usdt_bf]{usdt_bf()}}. Each test rejects H0 at level
\code{alpha}:
\itemize{
\item \code{q = c}: the two-sided test of H0: \eqn{q = c}.
\item \code{q > c} and \code{q < c}: one-sided tests. With a margin on the unfavourable
side of the reference value, \code{diff > -0.2} is a non-inferiority test and
\code{diff < 0.2} a non-superiority test.
\item \verb{q in [a, b]}: an equivalence test by two one-sided tests (TOST;
Schuirmann, 1987; Lakens et al., 2018). H0, \eqn{q \le a} or
\eqn{q \ge b}, is rejected when both one-sided tests reject their part of
it, so \code{p.value} is the larger of \code{p.lower} and \code{p.upper}.
\item \verb{q out [a, b]}: a minimal-effect test. H0, \eqn{a \le q \le b}, is
rejected when either one-sided test rejects it at \code{alpha / 2}, so
\code{p.value} is twice the smaller of \code{p.lower} and \code{p.upper}. As the region
shrinks to a point it becomes the two-sided test of that point.
}

Each test is decided by the position of a confidence interval, and the row
reports that interval: of level \code{1 - 2 * alpha} for the one-sided and
equivalence tests, and \code{1 - alpha} for the two-sided and minimal-effect
tests, which split \code{alpha} between two tails. With the default
\code{alpha = 0.05}, an equivalence test reports a 90\% interval, and H0 is
rejected exactly when that interval lies inside the region.
}

\section{The correlation}{
The bounds of \code{rho} are given, and its estimate and interval reported, on
the correlation scale. The test runs on the Fisher-\eqn{z} scale, where the
sampling distribution of the estimate is closer to normal:
\deqn{z = \frac{\mathrm{atanh}(\hat\rho) - \mathrm{atanh}(c)}{SE /
(1 - \hat\rho^2)}.}{z = (atanh(rho) - atanh(c)) / (SE / (1 - rho^2)).}
Because \code{atanh()} is increasing, \eqn{\rho > c} and
\eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(\rho) > \mathrm{atanh}(c)}{atanh(rho) > atanh(c)} are
the same statement, so the p-value and the decision are those of the
hypothesis about \code{rho}, and the interval, transformed back, stays inside
(-1, 1).

Every p-value comes from the interval reported with it. \code{\link[=usdt_tests]{usdt_tests()}}
instead tests a null association on the covariance that \code{rho} and the slope
share, so its p-values for \code{rho = 0} and \code{slope = 0} can differ slightly
from these.
}

\section{The intercept and the slope}{
Both divide by the variance of the direct sensitivity. When the direct
sensitivity barely varies across subjects, their standard errors are large
and their Wald intervals inaccurate, so their equivalence tests need many
subjects to succeed, and the bootstrap of \code{\link[=usdt_boot]{usdt_boot()}} gives more accurate
intervals.
}

\section{Boundary fits and the bootstrap}{
The correlation, slope and intercept need the full covariance matrix of the
fit, which is unavailable when the sensitivity covariance is on the
boundary (a variance estimated as zero, or a correlation of -1 or 1).
Their rows are then marked \code{"not estimable"}, while the difference, which
only needs the fixed effects, is still tested. After \code{\link[=usdt_boot]{usdt_boot()}}, every
test uses the bootstrap replicates and the interval type chosen there. The
p-value at a bound is the share of the bootstrap distribution beyond it,
counted as \eqn{(k + 1) / (B + 1)} as in \code{\link[=usdt_boot]{usdt_boot()}}, so the decision
agrees with the reported interval up to that correction.
}

\examples{
d <- usdt_data_tasks(
  direct   = vadillo_awareness,
  indirect = vadillo_cuing,
  subject_col      = "subj",
  condition_col    = "condition",
  condition_levels = c(signal = "old", noise = "new"),
  response_col     = list(direct = "judged.old", indirect = "rt"),
  response_levels  = list(direct   = c(signal = 1, noise = 0),
                          indirect = c(signal = "faster", noise = "slower")),
  dichotomize      = list(direct = FALSE, indirect = TRUE)
)

m <- hsdt(d)

# Mean sensitivities equivalent within 0.2 d' units, and an unconscious
# effect at zero awareness smaller than 0.2
usdt_equivalence(m, c("diff in [-0.2, 0.2]", "intercept < 0.2"))

# The bounds of the correlation are correlations
usdt_equivalence(m, "rho in [-0.3, 0.3]")

}
